sentences.l:
and among them stood up
the hero, Atreus’-son, wide-ruling Agamemnon,
grieving;

because I the maiden Chryseis’ splendid ransom
did not wish to accept, since much I prefer her
to keep at home;

for indeed, then, I prefer her to Clytemnestra,
the wedded wife, since she is not inferior to her,
not in body nor in stature, nor then in mind nor in any works.

but if they do not give, I myself shall take,
either yours or Ajax’s prize, going, or Odysseus’s,
I will carry off, having taken;

and in, suitably, oarsmen let us gather, and in, a hecatomb
let us put, and up Chryseis herself, fair-cheeked,
let us make go;

sentences.t:
ὤ μοι ἀναιδείην ἐπιειμένε κερδαλεόφρον
πῶς τίς τοι πρόφρων ἔπεσιν πείθηται Ἀχαιῶν
ἢ ὁδὸν ἐλθέμεναι ἢ ἀνδράσιν ἶφι μάχεσθαι;

sentences.tr:
ō moi anaideiēn epieimene kerdaleophron
pōs tis toi prophrōn epesin peithētai Achaiōn
ē hodon elthemenai ē andrasin iphi machesthai;

sentences.l:
for never yet did they drive off my cattle, nor my horses,
nor ever in Phthia, rich-soiled, man-nourishing,
harm the crop, since in truth very many things lie between,
the shadowy mountains and the resounding sea;

But you, O greatly shameless one, along we followed, that you might rejoice,
winning honour for Menelaus and for you, dog-faced one,
from the Trojans;

Homeward going with both your ships and your companions,
rule over the Myrmidons; but of you I do not care,
nor do I heed you bearing a grudge;

While he was pondering these things in mind and in heart,
and was drawing from its sheath the great sword, there came Athena
from heaven;
